WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.942
This lesson becomes much cleaner when acceleration is treated as a signed rate of change in velocity.

2
00:00:04.942 --> 00:00:09.884
Velocity itself can be positive or negative depending on the chosen direction.

3
00:00:09.884 --> 00:00:14.825
Acceleration inherits that signed logic because it describes how the velocity changes over time.

4
00:00:14.825 --> 00:00:19.767
So acceleration is not simply a synonym for speeding up.

5
00:00:19.767 --> 00:00:24.709
It is a directional rate of change.

6
00:00:24.709 --> 00:00:29.651
That is why the sign convention matters before the arithmetic begins.

7
00:00:29.651 --> 00:00:34.593
Keep this takeaway in view: Acceleration is not just speeding up; it is signed velocity change per unit time.

8
00:00:34.593 --> 00:00:39.535
Velocity means signed motion.

9
00:00:39.535 --> 00:00:44.476
Velocity already includes direction through its sign.

10
00:00:44.476 --> 00:00:49.418
Acceleration means signed change.

11
00:00:49.418 --> 00:00:54.360
Acceleration measures how that signed velocity changes over time.

12
00:00:54.360 --> 00:01:00.477
The velocity graph helps because it shows the axis carrying positive and negative values.

13
00:01:00.477 --> 00:01:06.593
If the velocity rises overall as time moves on, the slope is positive and the acceleration is positive.

14
00:01:06.593 --> 00:01:12.710
If it falls overall, the acceleration is negative.

15
00:01:12.710 --> 00:01:18.827
The graph also warns that crossing the axis does not break the rule.

16
00:01:18.827 --> 00:01:24.943
The sign still comes from the signed change over time.

17
00:01:24.943 --> 00:01:31.060
That makes the graph a useful bridge between the formal calculation and the physical meaning.

18
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:37.177
Keep this takeaway in view: The axes and the signed change decide the acceleration sign, not a vague feeling about the motion.

19
00:01:37.177 --> 00:01:43.293
Axis sign: The graph itself shows which velocities are positive and which are negative.

20
00:01:43.293 --> 00:01:49.410
Slope sign: The overall rise or fall gives the acceleration sign.

21
00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:53.692
A strong analogy is a before-and-after photo of a signed velocity arrow.

22
00:01:53.692 --> 00:01:57.975
The first photo shows the initial arrow.

23
00:01:57.975 --> 00:02:02.257
The second photo shows the final arrow.

24
00:02:02.257 --> 00:02:06.539
Acceleration is not either arrow on its own.

25
00:02:06.539 --> 00:02:10.822
It is the signed change between them divided by the time between the photos.

26
00:02:10.822 --> 00:02:15.104
If the arrow points the same way but gets shorter, the change may be negative in the chosen direction.

27
00:02:15.104 --> 00:02:19.386
If it grows more positive, the change is positive.

28
00:02:19.386 --> 00:02:23.668
This picture helps because it makes subtraction feel physical instead of symbolic.

29
00:02:23.668 --> 00:02:27.951
Keep this takeaway in view: Acceleration tracks the signed change between two velocity states.

30
00:02:27.951 --> 00:02:32.233
Photo one means initial velocity.

31
00:02:32.233 --> 00:02:36.515
Start with the signed velocity at the beginning.

32
00:02:36.515 --> 00:02:40.798
Photo two means final velocity.

33
00:02:40.798 --> 00:02:45.080
Then compare it with the signed velocity later.

34
00:02:45.080 --> 00:02:50.022
The method is acceleration equals change in velocity divided by time.

35
00:02:50.022 --> 00:02:54.963
In symbols, a equals v minus u over t.

36
00:02:54.963 --> 00:02:59.905
The most important habit is to write the velocities with signs before subtracting.

37
00:02:59.905 --> 00:03:04.846
Final minus initial must be done with the signs still attached.

38
00:03:04.846 --> 00:03:09.788
Only then should the result be divided by time.

39
00:03:09.788 --> 00:03:14.729
This prevents the common mistake of subtracting magnitudes and guessing the sign afterward.

40
00:03:14.729 --> 00:03:19.671
Keep this takeaway in view: Keep the signs through the subtraction.

41
00:03:19.671 --> 00:03:24.612
Do not bolt them on afterward.

42
00:03:24.612 --> 00:03:29.554
Good habit: Write +10 m/s and +4 m/s, not just 10 and 4, before subtracting.

43
00:03:29.554 --> 00:03:34.495
Bad habit: Subtract the magnitudes first and decide the sign by intuition afterward.

44
00:03:34.495 --> 00:03:39.437
a = change in velocity / time.

45
00:03:39.437 --> 00:03:44.378
a = (v - u) / t.

46
00:03:44.378 --> 00:03:49.320
Write the signed velocities first, then subtract, then divide by time.

47
00:03:49.320 --> 00:03:55.306
One of the most important ideas here is that the sign of acceleration and the change in speed are not always the same story.

48
00:03:55.306 --> 00:04:01.292
If the object is already moving in the negative direction and the acceleration is also negative, the speed can increase while the acceleration remains negative.

49
00:04:01.292 --> 00:04:07.278
Likewise, a positive acceleration can go with slowing down if the object is moving negatively.

50
00:04:07.278 --> 00:04:13.264
This is why the lesson insists on talking about signed velocity, not only about speed.

51
00:04:13.264 --> 00:04:19.250
Keep this takeaway in view: Acceleration sign is directional.

52
00:04:19.250 --> 00:04:25.236
Speed change needs the velocity sign too.

53
00:04:25.236 --> 00:04:31.222
Same sign means speed can increase.

54
00:04:31.222 --> 00:04:37.208
Velocity and acceleration in the same signed direction can produce speeding up.

55
00:04:37.208 --> 00:04:43.194
Opposite sign means speed can decrease.

56
00:04:43.194 --> 00:04:49.180
Velocity and acceleration in opposite signed directions can produce slowing down.

57
00:04:49.180 --> 00:04:55.343
A common trap is to say negative acceleration always means slowing down.

58
00:04:55.343 --> 00:05:01.505
That is only true in some sign situations.

59
00:05:01.505 --> 00:05:07.668
Negative acceleration means the acceleration points in the chosen negative direction.

60
00:05:07.668 --> 00:05:13.830
Whether the speed increases or decreases depends on the current velocity direction as well.

61
00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:19.992
The lesson therefore asks for more disciplined language: talk first about signed change, then about the resulting speed behavior if needed.

62
00:05:19.992 --> 00:05:26.155
Keep this takeaway in view: Signed acceleration needs signed thinking all the way through.

63
00:05:26.155 --> 00:05:32.318
Choose the positive direction first.

64
00:05:32.318 --> 00:05:38.480
Keep the signs on both velocities during subtraction.

65
00:05:38.480 --> 00:05:42.378
Check yourself.

66
00:05:42.378 --> 00:05:46.275
What must be chosen before the signs can be interpreted?

67
00:05:46.275 --> 00:05:50.173
A positive direction.

68
00:05:50.173 --> 00:05:54.071
What subtraction gives the change in velocity?

69
00:05:54.071 --> 00:05:57.968
Final minus initial.

70
00:05:57.968 --> 00:06:01.866
And if the result is negative, what does that guarantee?

71
00:06:01.866 --> 00:06:05.764
Only that the acceleration points in the chosen negative direction.

72
00:06:05.764 --> 00:06:09.662
Those answers are enough to make the worked example feel much safer.

73
00:06:09.662 --> 00:06:13.559
Keep this takeaway in view: Sign convention first, subtraction second, interpretation third.

74
00:06:13.559 --> 00:06:17.457
First step means choose positive.

75
00:06:17.457 --> 00:06:21.355
The sign story depends on the coordinate choice.

76
00:06:21.355 --> 00:06:25.252
Change rule means v - u.

77
00:06:25.252 --> 00:06:29.150
Final minus initial gives the signed velocity change.

78
00:06:29.150 --> 00:06:33.952
You are ready for the worked example now.

79
00:06:33.952 --> 00:06:38.753
Take east as positive.

80
00:06:38.753 --> 00:06:43.555
The cyclist changes from plus ten metres per second to plus four metres per second in three seconds.

81
00:06:43.555 --> 00:06:48.356
Keep the signs on the velocities.

82
00:06:48.356 --> 00:06:53.158
Change in velocity equals four minus ten, which is minus six metres per second.

83
00:06:53.158 --> 00:06:57.959
Then divide by the time: minus six divided by three gives minus two metres per second squared.

84
00:06:57.959 --> 00:07:02.761
The negative sign means the acceleration points west, opposite the chosen positive direction.

85
00:07:02.761 --> 00:07:07.562
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: signed change first, then divide, then interpret.

86
00:07:07.562 --> 00:07:12.364
Step 1: Choose the positive direction and write the signed velocities.

87
00:07:12.364 --> 00:07:17.165
Step 2: Find the signed velocity change with final minus initial.

88
00:07:17.165 --> 00:07:21.967
change in velocity = 4 - 10 = -6 m/s.

89
00:07:21.967 --> 00:07:26.768
a = -6 / 3.

90
00:07:26.768 --> 00:07:31.570
The negative sign belongs to the signed change in velocity and the chosen direction.
